      "text": "Given that \\(\\cos R = \\frac{4}{9}\\) in the right triangle \\(SQR\\) with \\(SR = 9\\), we need to find the length of \\(QS\\).\n\nIn a right triangle, the cosine of an angle is the ratio of the length of the adjacent side to the hypotenuse. Here, \\(\\cos R = \\frac{QR}{SR}\\). Let \\(QR = x\\). Since \\(\\cos R = \\frac{4}{9}\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\cos R = \\frac{QR}{SR} = \\frac{x}{9} = \\frac{4}{9}\n\\]\n\nFrom this, we can solve for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x}{9} = \\frac{4}{9} \\implies x = 4\n\\]\n\nSo, \\(QR = 4\\). Now, we need to find the length of \\(QS\\). In the right triangle \\(SQR\\), we use the Pythagorean theorem to find \\(SQ\\):\n\n\\[\nSQ^2 + QR^2 = SR^2\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the known values:\n\n\\[\nSQ^2 + 4^2 = 9^2\n\\]\n\n\\[\nSQ^2 + 16 = 81\n\\]\n\n\\[\nSQ^2 = 81 - 16\n\\]\n\n\\[\nSQ^2 = 65\n\\]\n\n\\[\nSQ = \\sqrt{65}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the length of \\(QS\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\sqrt{65}}\\).",
