      "text": "To solve for \\( QS \\) given that \\(\\cos R = \\frac{4}{9}\\) in the right triangle \\( \\triangle RSQ \\) with \\( SR = 9 \\), we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the sides of the triangle**: \n   - \\( SR \\) is one leg of the right triangle.\n   - \\( RQ \\) is the hypotenuse.\n   - \\( QS \\) is the other leg of the right triangle.\n\n2. **Use the cosine function**: \n   The cosine of an angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the adjacent side to the hypotenuse. Here, \\(\\cos R = \\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\frac{RQ}{SR}\\).\n\n3. **Set up the equation**:\n   \\[\n   \\cos R = \\frac{RQ}{SR} = \\frac{RQ}{9}\n   \\]\n   Given \\(\\cos R = \\frac{4}{9}\\), we can substitute this value into the equation:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{RQ}{9} = \\frac{4}{9}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solve for \\( RQ \\)**:\n   \\[\n   RQ = 4\n   \\]\n\n5. **Determine the length of \\( QS \\)**:\n   Since \\( Q \\) and \\( S \\) form a right angle, \\( \\triangle RSQ \\) is a right triangle. The length of \\( QS \\) is the other leg opposite the angle \\( R \\). We can use the Pythagorean theorem to find \\( QS \\):\n   \\[\n   RQ^2 = QS^2 + SR^2\n   \\]\n   Substituting the known values:\n   \\[\n   4^2 = QS^2 + 9^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   16 = QS^2 + 81\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( QS^2 \\):\n   \\[\n   QS^2 = 16 - 81\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   QS^2 = -65\n   \\]\n\n6. **Revisit the problem**:\n   The negative value indicates an error in the interpretation. Let's re-evaluate the problem. The correct interpretation should be:\n   \\[\n   QS^2 = 9^2 - 4^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   QS^2 = 81 - 16\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   QS^2 = 65\n   \\]\n   Taking the square root of both sides:\n   \\[\n   QS = \\sqrt{65}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the length of \\( QS \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\sqrt{65}}\\).",
