      "text": "To find the length of \\( QS \\), we can use the cosine value of angle \\( R \\) in the right triangle \\( SQR \\).\n\nGiven:\n- \\( \\cos R = \\frac{4}{9} \\)\n- \\( SR = 9 \\)\n\nWe know that:\n\\[\n\\cos R = \\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\frac{SR}{QR}\n\\]\nSubstitute the given values:\n\\[\n\\frac{4}{9} = \\frac{9}{QR}\n\\]\nSolve for \\( QR \\):\n\\[\nQR = \\frac{9 \\times 9}{4} = \\frac{81}{4}\n\\]\n\nNow, we use the Pythagorean theorem to find the length of \\( SQ \\). In right triangle \\( SQR \\), the Pythagorean theorem states:\n\\[\nQR^2 = QS^2 + SR^2\n\\]\nSubstitute the known values:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{81}{4}\\right)^2 = QS^2 + 9^2\n\\]\nCalculate \\( \\left(\\frac{81}{4}\\right)^2 \\):\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{81}{4}\\right)^2 = \\frac{6561}{16}\n\\]\nAnd \\( 9^2 \\):\n\\[\n9^2 = 81\n\\]\nSo the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{6561}{16} = QS^2 + 81\n\\]\nConvert 81 to a fraction with a denominator of 16:\n\\[\n81 = \\frac{1296}{16}\n\\]\nThus, the equation is:\n\\[\n\\frac{6561}{16} = QS^2 + \\frac{1296}{16}\n\\]\nSubtract \\(\\frac{1296}{16}\\) from both sides:\n\\[\n\\frac{6561}{16} - \\frac{1296}{16} = QS^2\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{5265}{16} = QS^2\n\\]\nTake the square root of both sides:\n\\[\nQS = \\sqrt{\\frac{5265}{16}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{5265}}{4}\n\\]\nTherefore, the length of \\( QS \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{5265}}{4}}\n\\]",
